   "detail": "Before the fix (code at commit fb8a6cb, lines 575-578 of the then tools/origination.py), a side bet was graded by comparing the straight-up margin with the pick, ignoring the selected team's closing line. Week 9, Browns at Saints (ESPN event 401873039): against its own closing line Cleveland (margin -1, plus 4.5) covers and the bet is won, but the recorded row said lost. Official NFL.com final re-read live on 2026-11-18: CLE 22 - NO 23, overtime (see official_url). Fixed on main in PR #63 (commit 915bf42): settlement now grades the official margin plus the recorded home handicap, with an exact tie as a push, and three regression tests were added (tools/test_origination.py, run by the publication gate). The recorded row now says won. Re-verified 2026-11-18 by this session: tools/settlement_recheck.py on the current tree reports 24 settled bets (Weeks 9-10), 0 changed outcomes, recorded = re-settled; the published signal-vs-staking.html shows +15.414 units and +64.224% ROI per bet on n=24 and luck.html shows the same 15.41 units - the old claim that an uncorrected figure was still published is no longer true. The pre-fix row survives in git history at fb8a6cb, so the original record stays auditable.",
   "why": "This is a settlement error, not a change of rule, so it is corrected rather than argued. Caveats that still stand: n = 24 bets over eight games - neither ROI is evidence of skill, a game-cluster interval would span zero, and the prices are one book's (DraftKings as published by ESPN, third-party, see F-01), not official lines. Reproducible: tools/settlement_recheck.py re-settles every bet from the committed files without reusing tools/luck.py, and tools/test_origination.py pins the rule.",
